    "original_title": "DFS Iyo Waqooyi Bari oo Adkeeyey Qaadista Hubka Ee Duullimaadyada Laascaanood",
    "slug": "officials-tighten-weapons-transport-rules-on-mogadishu-las-anod-flights",
    "summary": "Authorities in Mogadishu and the North East State have introduced new measures requiring advance notification and permits for transporting weapons on flights between the two regions.",
    "content": "Mogadishu's Aden Adde Airport authority and the North East State Ministry of Security have jointly issued new directives aimed at strengthening control over weapons carried on flights connecting Mogadishu and Las Anod. The measures prohibit airlines from transporting armed passengers or military personnel without prior authorization from the ministry.\n\nThe North East State Ministry of Security has specifically barred carriers from allowing any armed individuals or forces on board unless they have obtained advance clearance. Under the new rules, any person holding a legally registered firearm must notify the ministry at least three days before their journey and present documentation for the weapon.\n\nIn a related instruction, the Aden Adde Airport management has directed the operators of the Reflector terminal to enforce the new procedures in coordination with security agencies. The airport authority said the steps are intended to prevent unauthorized weapons from moving through civilian aviation channels.\n\nThese developments follow growing concerns about the movement of arms between southern and northern regions, particularly as Las Anod has been the center of prolonged conflict. The involvement of both federal and North East State authorities signals a coordinated effort to regulate air travel that has often been used for both civilian and military purposes.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the new restrictions could affect travel plans involving legal firearms, as passengers may now face additional paperwork and delays. Many in the diaspora frequently fly between Mogadishu and Las Anod to visit family or conduct business, and the requirement to notify authorities three days in advance may require adjustments to itineraries.\n\nThe directives also highlight the broader challenge of managing security in a fragmented political landscape, where different administrations control key airports and transport routes. Compliance will depend on effective communication between airlines, terminal operators, and both regional and federal authorities.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/10/dfs-iyo-waqooyi-bari-oo-adkeeyey-qaadista-hubka-ee-duullimaadyada-laascaanood/",
