    "title": "US Military Warns Houthis May Expand Operations into Somalia",
    "original_title": "AFRICOM oo Ka Digay In Xuutiyiintu Hawlgalladooda Ku Fidin karaan Soomaaliya",
    "slug": "us-military-warns-houthis-may-expand-operations-into-somalia",
    "summary": "AFRICOM has issued a warning that Houthi fighters may be extending their activities into Somalia, potentially opening a new front for attacks on shipping and US forces in the region.",
    "content": "The United States Africa Command (AFRICOM) has issued a warning that Yemen's Houthi movement may be attempting to expand its operations into Somalia. According to an AFRICOM official, who spoke on condition of anonymity due to briefing protocols, there are indications that Houthi fighters or proxies could already be present in northern Somalia and may have established contacts with the militant group Al-Shabaab.\n\nThe official stated that the full extent of the Houthi presence in Somalia is not yet fully understood. The warning comes amid heightened tensions in the Red Sea and Gulf of Aden, where Houthi forces have been conducting attacks on commercial shipping for months. The group has also targeted US military assets in the region, prompting defensive strikes by American forces.\n\nThe Houthis, who control large parts of Yemen, have been engaged in a protracted conflict with a Saudi-led coalition since 2015. Their maritime campaign has disrupted global trade routes, forcing many vessels to reroute around the southern tip of Africa. The possibility of the group opening a new front in Somalia raises concerns about a broader regional escalation.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is particularly significant. Many have family members in Somalia and are already concerned about the ongoing threat posed by Al-Shabaab. A potential Houthi presence could further destabilize the region, potentially leading to increased military activity and civilian displacement. Diaspora communities have also been vital in supporting reconstruction and development efforts, and any new security threat could jeopardize those gains.\n\nAFRICOM's warning underscores the need for continued vigilance and international cooperation to prevent the conflict from spreading. The US military has maintained a presence in Somalia, primarily focused on training and advising local forces in their fight against Al-Shabaab. The prospect of Houthi involvement adds a new dimension to an already complex security landscape.\n\nAs of now, there has been no official response from Somali authorities or Houthi representatives regarding these claims. The situation remains fluid, and further developments are expected as more information becomes available.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/10/africom-oo-ka-digay-in-xuutiyiintu-hawlgalladooda-ku-fidin-karaan-soomaaliya/",
